## 3. Business case

**Formula**

```
minutes saved / month = orders × (t_before − t_after)
t_after               = a × t_ready_check + (1 − a) × t_review
FTE hours freed       = minutes saved / 60
FTEs                  = FTE hours freed / 140 productive hours per FTE-month
```

| Input | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| orders / month | 1,500 | (A) |
| t_before: hands-on minutes per order today | 18 | (A) |
| a: share auto-routed Ready | {{AUTO_RATE}} | (M) PAP, at {{THRESHOLD}} |
| t_ready_check: spot check of an auto-ready order | 1.5 min | (A) |
| t_review: reviewer time on a flagged order, with findings and evidence pre-highlighted | 6 min | (A), validate in shadow mode |
| pipeline time per order | {{LATENCY}} median including OCR, unattended | (M) |
| model cost per order | {{COST}} | (M) |

**Result (with the inputs above):** t_after = {{T_AFTER}} min. Minutes saved per month =
{{MIN_SAVED}}, which is **{{HOURS}} hours, or {{FTES}} FTEs**, redeployed to outreach and
patient setup. Model spend is {{MONTHLY_COST}} a month.

**Orders cleared faster.** Incomplete orders get a drafted, cited request the same hour
they arrive instead of after a coordinator reaches them in the inbox.

**Denials avoided (A).** If 5% of orders currently ship with a documentation defect, every
defect the engine catches before dispensing is a denial or recoupment avoided. On the
synthetic set it caught {{RECALL}} of planted defects (M); real-world recall on their own
faxes is exactly what phase 0 measures, so size this line with their denial log then.

